.TH SPARKEY 1 2026-10-14 "sparkey @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sparkey \- create, inspect and read sparkey key/value files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sparkey info
.I file
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B sparkey get
.I index.spi key
.br
.B sparkey writehash
.I log.spl
.br
.B sparkey createlog
.RB [ \-c
.IR compression ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR blocksize ]
.I log.spl
.br
.B sparkey appendlog
.RB [ \-d
.IR delimiter ]
.I log.spl
.br
.B sparkey rewrite
.RB [ \-c
.IR compression ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR blocksize ]
.I input.spi output.spi
.br
.B sparkey help
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sparkey is a simple constant key/value store, suited to read-heavy workloads
with infrequent bulk writes.
Data is stored in two files: an append-only log file
.RI ( .spl )
holding the key/value entries, and an immutable index file
.RI ( .spi )
holding a hash table that points into the log.
.PP
.B sparkey
is a command line tool for creating, inspecting and reading these files.
Commands that take an index file find the matching log file by replacing the
.I .spi
extension with
.IR .spl ,
and vice versa.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B info \fIfile\fR ...
Print the header information of each file, which may be either a log or an
index file.
.TP
.B get \fIindex.spi key\fR
Look up
.I key
and write its value to standard output, exactly as stored and without a
trailing newline.
.TP
.B writehash \fIlog.spl\fR
Build an index file for
.IR log.spl ,
replacing any existing index file of the same name.
Run this after appending to a log so that lookups see the new entries.
.TP
.B createlog \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIlog.spl\fR
Create a new, empty log file.
.TP
.B appendlog \fR[\fB\-d \fIdelimiter\fR] \fIlog.spl\fR
Read records from standard input and append them to an existing log file.
Each record is a line of the form
.IR key " " delimiter " " value ,
terminated by a newline.
The line is split on the first delimiter, so the value may itself contain the
delimiter.
The last line does not need a trailing newline.
Empty keys and empty values are not supported, and neither are lines longer
than 256 MiB.
If a later record uses the same key as an earlier one, the later value
replaces it.
.TP
.B rewrite \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIinput.spi output.spi\fR
Copy all live entries of an index/log pair into a new pair, dropping replaced
and deleted entries.
This can also change the compression settings.
The input and output must be different files.
.TP
.B help
Show a summary of the commands.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " compression"
Compression algorithm for the log file:
.BR none ,
.B snappy
or
.BR zstd .
For
.BR createlog ,
the default is
.BR none .
For
.BR rewrite ,
the default is to keep the input's setting.
.TP
.BI \-b " blocksize"
Compression block size in bytes, between 16 and 1073741824.
For
.BR createlog ,
the default is 4096.
For
.BR rewrite ,
the default is to keep the input's setting, so this option must be given when
compressing a log that was previously uncompressed.
Larger blocks usually compress better but make lookups slower, since each
lookup decompresses a whole block.
.TP
.BI \-d " delimiter"
The single character separating keys from values in
.B appendlog
input.
The default is a tab.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B get
exits with 0 if the key was found, 1 on error and 2 if the key was not found.
The other commands exit with 0 on success and 1 on error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a log, add two entries, build the index and look up a key:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sparkey createlog \-c zstd data.spl
$ printf 'alice\et1\enbob\et2\en' | sparkey appendlog data.spl
$ sparkey writehash data.spl
$ sparkey get data.spi alice
1
.RE
.fi
.PP
Remove replaced entries and switch to Snappy compression:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sparkey rewrite \-c snappy \-b 4096 data.spi compact.spi
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The sparkey README and API documentation:
.br
\%https://github.com/pilate/sparkey
